ical pre-Seven Years' War period.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-09-28T21:35:26Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#36484705123", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Gaston-Pierre-Charles de Lévis-Lomagne, duc de Mirepoix, maréchal de France (1757) and Ambassador of Louis XV, was a French aristocrat.", "newValue": "Gaston-Pierre-Charles de Lévis-Lomagne, duc de Mirepoix, born on December 2, 1699, in Belleville, was a distinguished French aristocrat who ascended to the highest echelons of military and diplomatic service under King Louis XV. His career, spanning the mid-18th century, positioned him as a key figure in French foreign policy and military command during a tumultuous period for European powers. Elevated to Marshal of France in 1757, the year of his death, Mirepoix embodied the intricate blend of aristocratic privilege, military prowess, and diplomatic acumen characteristic of the Ancien Régime.

His early military career saw him participate actively in the War of the Polish Succession (1733–1738) and the War of the Austrian Succession (1740–1748), where he distinguished himself through various commands. Notably, he served as a lieutenant-general in the campaign of Bohemia and later in Flanders, demonstrating strategic competence. Beyond the battlefield, Mirepoix undertook crucial diplomatic missions, notably as Ambassador to Vienna from 1739 to 1742 and subsequently to London from 1749 to 1751. These postings placed him at the heart of European political maneuvering, where he worked to advance French interests amidst shifting alliances and rivalries, particularly with the Habsburg Monarchy and Great Britain.

